id: conversation_1
translator_note: "Quotes are verbatim from participants. region and field values are data keys; do not translate them."
quote_1: "I think that the state of California should make it illegal to fire people to be replaced by AI. And then make the standards very strict and the punishment for doing so severe. Very severe."
attribution_1: Finance worker from LA
region_1: Los Angeles
field_1: Financial
quote_2: "I'd say within government, just shifting people within the organization to other underserved areas instead of just cutting people wholesale: I think, would be the way to go."
attribution_2: Healthcare worker from LA
region_2: Los Angeles
field_2: Healthcare
quote_3: "I'm concerned about the thoughts of tightly regulating industries, as far as telling them who they can hire, who they can fire. As a government entity that is something that we should be very careful of: exerting that much control over private industry."
attribution_3: Government worker from the Sierra region
region_3: Sierra Region
field_3: Government
----

----
id: conversation_2
quote_1: "I think the general concern is AI tools right now can be used very easily to deceive. And while, it is one of the grandest freedoms in the United States: you're allowed to be deceptive. But a little watermark on things, just so people can activate their skepticism: Maybe not the worst thing in the world."
attribution_1: Construction worker in the Sacramento valley / Sierra foothills
region_1: Sacramento Valley / Sierra Foothills
field_1: Goods producing and harvesting
quote_2: "Require AI to identify itself and what it generates."
attribution_2: IT worker from Southern California
region_2: Southern California
field_2: Information technology
quote_3: "Sorry, I have a slight disagreement. I think that it should be the responsibility of companies who are selling a product to identify that they are using AI in their advertising and not just the AI itself."
attribution_3: Unemployed Californian from LA
region_3: Los Angeles
field_3: "Unemployed, looking"
----

----
id: conversation_4
quote_1: "Recently, in my own community, there was the potential of a data center being built, and my own community wasn't notified, wasn't told at all. Making sure that our local governments are responsible to our citizens for the transparency regarding building and construction and implementation of data centers would be a helpful policy."
attribution_1: Arts/media/entertainment worker from the far north/north coast of California
region_1: Far North / North Coast
field_1: "Arts, entertainment, or media"
quote_2: "I just want to piggyback off that point. I think probably holding town halls, or at least bringing it up in some forum where the community can understand the pros and cons of a data center there."
attribution_2: Government worker from the Bay Area
region_2: Bay Area
field_2: Government
----

----
id: conversation_8
quote_1: "AI is not currently subject to existing liability laws, and this is just due to some very outdated technicalities with how the laws are written. For example, there is ambiguity on whether or not AI software or deployed software qualifies as a product. This is because the software does not come packaged in hardware. That is something that the state can very easily just eliminate as an ambiguity by making sure it is defined."
attribution_1: Arts/entertainment/media worker from LA
region_1: Los Angeles
field_1: "Arts, entertainment, or media"
quote_2: "I appreciate all the homework you've done. That is always cool to see. Love participating. I think that liability is at the top with most votes for a very simple reason. I think that it's kind of a knock-on effect. If AI companies and AI purveyors and AI people who make the algorithms are held liable for everything that's down in that list below liability, I think that the problem kind of solves itself."
attribution_2: Education worker from Southern California
region_2: Southern California
field_2: Education
----

----
id: conversation_14
quote_1: "I agree with your point there on data tracking. Another thing I think we should protect is also our natural resources, and a clean environment, because I know people in Texas, where data farms or data centers have moved in, and the water has been polluted, and electric bills go way up, so yeah, protecting our natural resources."
attribution_1: Arts/entertainment/media worker from Southern California
region_1: Southern California
field_1: "Arts, entertainment, or media"
quote_2: "I think for me, and I know the previous speaker kind of touched on it, is the environmental impacts. I think for me, that's my biggest concern, is just the amount of water and electricity the AI is using. You hear of the data center in Georgia that illegally tapped into some city’s or county's water supply and took millions of gallons from it without anybody realizing."
attribution_2: Government worker from LA
region_2: Los Angeles
field_2: Government
quote_3: "I mean, I think that when I think about the state as a physical place, we could set environmental regulations, we could set data privacy requirements for states that operate here. When I look at this list, I get really excited about what floor are we setting for environmental protection when it comes to energy use, space use, etc."
attribution_3: Government worker from the Bay Area
region_3: Bay Area
field_3: Government
