----
id: what_happens_next
----
## What happens next

The ideas Californians shared don't end with this report. They join a fast-moving policy conversation already underway across the state.

As Engaged California's findings were being finalized, the Newsom Administration took action on AI. Several actions touch on themes that people raised in these discussions, among them the environmental footprint of data centers, transparency about AI-generated content, independent oversight of frontier models, and protections for workers and children.

A few recent examples:

- On September 16, the Governor signed Senate Bill 1050 by Senator Angelique Ashby, [requiring explicit disclosure](https://www.gov.ca.gov/2026/09/16/governor-newsom-signs-new-law-to-protect-workers-require-disclosures-on-ai-generated-advertising/) when AI-generated performers appear in any video or audio advertisement selling a product or service.
- On September 18, the Governor issued an Executive Order to accelerate the state’s [AI oversight](https://www.gov.ca.gov/2026/09/18/governor-newsom-issues-executive-order-to-accelerate-independent-oversight-and-advance-the-creation-of-an-ai-kill-switch/) efforts, fast-tracking new independent AI auditing laws, convening a panel of experts, and advancing an emergency "kill switch" for frontier AI models.
- On September 21, the Governor signed a package described as the nation's most comprehensive [data center laws](https://www.gov.ca.gov/2026/09/21/governor-newsom-signs-most-comprehensive-data-center-laws-in-the-nation-providing-communities-more-control-on-water-electricity-and-land-use/), giving communities more say over water, electricity, and land use.
- Earlier in September, the Governor signed [child safety laws for chatbots and social media](https://www.gov.ca.gov/2026/09/10/governor-newsom-signs-the-strongest-child-safety-chatbot-and-social-media-laws-in-the-nation/) and a set of [first-in-the-nation AI safeguards](https://www.gov.ca.gov/2026/09/09/governor-newsom-signs-first-in-the-nation-ai-safeguards-to-protect-californians-calls-on-the-federal-government-to-do-its-part/).

Our work isn’t done either. Californians raised many ideas that will keep informing government action in the months and years ahead as the administration transitions, the state legislature enters a new session, and the AI landscape keeps evolving.
